A||||12||1988LRC||EXTREMELY HARD steering||||1||
Z||000000||1988LRC||09-05-2001||12:59 PM||||The wife's 1988 coupe now has extremely hard steering had redone struts, tires, brakes, steering rack bushing, etc. and it has been working great with no problems in the past couple of days it has developed a problem where it is almost impossible to turn the wheel even with the front end off the ground and the tires removed (passenger side tire had to be beat off with hammer) at the very last bit of steering before hitting the stop it frees up and feels like normal so what happened? I think the wife has slammed it into a pothole or curb (says no, yeah, right) and ruined the rack any ideas or possibilities? are complete racks available? I'm really stumped||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||09-05-2001||01:08 PM||||Check the tie rod ends. See if they are bent.<BR>If the rack is bad you can get reman's at the Fiero store. Does it stick turning one way only? If both ways it could be the bushings were put in wrong or the rack is bad.||||reg||1
Z||000002||1988LRC||09-05-2001||01:37 PM||||Thanks for the reply, no the tie rods aren't bent goes down road fine just can't hardly turn the wheel, can't find any damage and the replacement aluminum bushing (Rodney) was installed correctly about 10k miles ago this has just developed could it need lubrication where the steel rack goes through the aluminum bushing? can I drill a hole in the rack and install a zerk? you can't hardly turn the wheel even when the front end is in the air but it dosen't seem to be binding or stripped I had the rack out to put the bushing in and would just as soon burn the damn car rather than than have to take the rack out again||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||09-05-2001||01:40 PM||||Gee, I'm really glad to read this considering that I am going to install the rack bushing + inner + outer tie rod ends this Saturday.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"><P>All I can think of is that the bushing might have come loose.  How'd you secure it to the rack?||||reg||1
Z||000004||1988LRC||09-05-2001||01:47 PM||||I secured it with the two pop rivets just like it said in the instructions and I don't think it came loose or there would be some play somewhere everything works fine and looks good it's just hard as hell to turn the wheel||||reg||1
Z||000005||skitime||09-05-2001||03:06 PM||||Start trouble shooting.  Put the front on stands.  Remove wheels.  Pop off one tie rod end. Test. Pop off other tie rod end. Test.  If it still turns hard remove and repair rack.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||1988LRC||09-05-2001||03:52 PM||||UPDATE . . PROBLEM SOLVED!!!!<BR>After disconnecting both outer tie rods noticed no difference in resistance loosened the right side boot band and discovered that even though I had lubed the new aluminum bushing it was now dry and harder to turn than it would be with 2 flat tires so a liberal application of grease and retighten the band and everything is great again. Now I have to drive it to go get the wife my sincerest token of apology. Guess I'll have the crow for dinner toninght.||||reg||14
Z||000007||skitime||09-05-2001||04:31 PM||||Glad to hear it.  At least you are owning up to your mistake.  haha  Maybe you could take her out to eat since crow probably won't be on the menu.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||09-05-2001||10:03 PM||||Glad your problem is solved!!!  So the aluminum bushing needs periodic lubrication?  What a pain in the arse.  Maybe I will try replacing just the inner + outer tie rod ends and see if that tightens things up without the new bushing.||||reg||11
Z||000009||theogre||09-05-2001||11:21 PM||||Just what lubricant was used? In this kind of thing the choice of grease is a subtle thing that can make a huge difference. The wrong grease can be really nasty about a number of things.||||reg||1
Z||000010||ChadMan||09-06-2001||08:47 AM||||Not to sound like a smartass...but what is the right grease?  I honestly don't know, and it would be nice to know before Saturday morning!||||reg||11
Z||000011||roadwarrior||09-06-2001||09:41 AM||||my guess on the right type of greese would be a bearing/chassis grease||||reg||1
Z||000012||rodmcneill||09-06-2001||11:43 AM||||I wish Rodney would make this bushing out of something else besides aluminum, in my opinion not a good material for a bushing. A bronze bushing with a grease fitting like the Fiero Store's rebuilt rack would be nice. On some 88's the original is steel and that wears out. I have a bearing service co. close to me he gave me catalogs on companies that make just bushings. I think when I replace mine I will get one the closest size and have my brother machine it. Has anyone checked to see if the Fiero Store will sell just the bronze bushings with grease fittings?||||reg||1
